            <div xml:id="d640">

                <pb facs="0778" n="690" sameAs="frus:frus1933v05/pg690"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">713.1311/185: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Sack</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-10-31T17:00:00-05:00">October 31, 1933—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>30. Your despatch No. 5, October 20, and telegram 34, October 28, 3 p.m.<anchor
                        n="16" xml:id="d640-f1"/> As the Department stated in its telegram No. 44 of
                    November 22, [1932] 7 p.m.,<anchor n="17" xml:id="d640-f2"/> to the Legation in
                    Guatemala the decision in the matter of the denunciation, modification or
                    continuance of the Treaty of Peace and Amity appears to be one for the Central
                    American Governments themselves to determine. We feel that Costa Rica has freely
                    exercised its right to denounce the Treaty and therefore do not desire that you
                    should even unofficially suggest that it rescind its action or otherwise
                    indicate what course of action it should pursue with respect to the Treaty.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
